<SiteLayout File="privacy.html" Title="Privacy"
            Description="What Ancilla does with your data: it stays on your computer, and nothing is sent anywhere but Halo's own services.">
    <div class="page-head">
        <div>
            <h1>Privacy</h1>
            <p>What Ancilla does with your data, in plain words. The app's About page says the same.</p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
        <section class="card" aria-labelledby="short">
            <h2 id="short">The short version</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>Ancilla runs on your computer. There's no account to make and no server of ours.</li>
                <li>It only talks to Halo's and Xbox's own services, signed in as you, and only sees what Halo Waypoint would show you.</li>
                <li>What it loads is kept on your computer, and is never sent anywhere else.</li>
                <li>There are no ads, no analytics and no tracking, in the app or on this website.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="card" aria-labelledby="token">
            <h2 id="token">Your Spartan token</h2>
            <p>
                The token Halo Waypoint gives you when you sign in is held in the app's memory while you're signed in. Ancilla
                never writes it to a file, never hands it to the page's scripts, and sends it nowhere except Halo's own
                services. Tokens run out after a few hours.
            </p>
            <p>
                Signing in with Microsoft happens on Halo Waypoint's own sign-in page, in a window with a browser profile of
                its own, so your password goes only to Microsoft. Halo Waypoint keeps the token there as a cookie, and Ancilla
                reads it from there. With <strong>Stay signed in</strong> on, that profile is kept on your computer so you're
                signed in when the app starts. <a href="getting-started.html#stay">Getting started</a> says where. Signing out, or turning Stay signed in off, removes it.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section class="card" aria-labelledby="kept">
            <h2 id="kept">What's kept on your computer</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <strong>Your match archive:</strong> each match you've played, its full scoreboard (which includes the IDs,
                    scores and medals of everyone in it), your skill result, map, mode and playlist names, and snapshots of your
                    progress, which power the matches-to-go figures. It's filed under your player ID, so another account's data
                    stays separate. You can export it from Settings.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Theater films</strong>, only when you open a match's timeline and choose to load it. A film holds the
                    gamertags of everyone in the match. When the cache goes over its size limit (2 GB unless you pick another in Settings), the films fetched longest ago are deleted, and Settings can clear it.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Log files</strong>, to help with bug reports: calls to Halo's services, and any errors and where in the app they happened. Tokens, player IDs, gamertags, download addresses and
                    file paths are removed before anything is written. They're kept for a week.
                </li>
                <li><strong>Your theme choice</strong>, in the app's settings.</li>
                <li><strong>Your settings</strong>, such as the film cache limit, in a small file written only once you change one.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                Settings lists all of these, with where each is kept and how big it is. <strong>Export everything</strong> saves
                your matches, progress snapshots and settings as one JSON file, without other players' names or IDs.
                <strong>Delete everything</strong>, after asking you to confirm, signs you out and deletes all of it from your computer.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section class="card" aria-labelledby="internet">
            <h2 id="internet">What the app loads from the internet</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>Your stats, matches and Theater films, from the services behind halowaypoint.com.</li>
                <li>Map thumbnails from Halo's content servers, and gamerpics from Xbox's image servers.</li>
                <li>The fonts for the Halo and XBOX themes, from Google Fonts. The Basic theme doesn't need them.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>Nothing else. Ancilla doesn't collect usage data or crash reports, and if it ever does, it will ask first and be off unless you turn it on.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="card" aria-labelledby="site">
            <h2 id="site">This website</h2>
            <p>
                This website has no cookies, no scripts, no analytics and no embedded content from anywhere else. It's hosted
                by GitHub Pages, and GitHub may log visitors' IP addresses for security, as described in the
                <a href="https://docs.github.com/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement">GitHub General Privacy Statement</a>.
                Download links go to GitHub Releases.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section class="card" aria-labelledby="contact">
            <h2 id="contact">Questions</h2>
            <p>Ask on <a href="@SiteModel.Issues">GitHub Issues</a>. Please don't include your token, or other players' gamertags.</p>
        </section>
    </div>
</SiteLayout>

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public SiteModel Site { get; set; } = null!;
}
